STEP 2 -- "show mirror interface <port>"                                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: run as drafted first, then with the argument the platform actually takes.

x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.11
x960-56YSQ#
                                           <-- EMPTY for the DESTINATION port

The DUT's own help says the argument is the SOURCE interface:

x960-56YSQ#show mirror ?
  interface  Source Interface to use

x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.10
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.11
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: transmit
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.10

Corroborating config read, showing where the mirror is stored:

x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.11
  !
  interface port1.0.11
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   mirror interface port1.0.10 direction transmit
  !
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  !
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
                                           <-- asymmetry is correct: the mirror is configured
                                               ON the destination and names the source; the
                                               source port carries no mirror configuration

  => STEP 2 PASS: `show mirror interface` reports the mirror correctly. On the x960 it is keyed
     on the MONITORED/source interface, so the destination form returns empty -- that is the
     command's argument convention, not a reporting failure. The drafted step named the wrong
     port.
